<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yaqub al-Mansur</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1184">1184</a>–1199</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> · Also known as Abu Yusuf Yaqub al-Mansur</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yaqub al-Mansur was the third <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a>, reigning from 1184 to 1199. He won a great victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-alarcos">Battle of Alarcos</a> in 1195, earning the title al-Mansur, 'made victorious by God', and raided as far as the walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>. His reign is considered the height of Almohad power, marked by major building projects and patronage of scholars.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yaqub-al-mansur.jpg" alt="Yaqub al-Mansur" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: David Stanley from Nanaimo, Canada · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tour_Hassan_(6858227583).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The caliphate's military power was at its greatest under Caliph <b>Abu Yusuf Yaqub al-Mansur</b> (r. 1184–1199), who won the empire's most celebrated victory at the Battle of Alarcos on 18 July 1195, where his forces crushed the Castilian army of Alfonso VIII.</blockquote><a href="/empire/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The Almohad caliph <b>Abu Yusuf Yaqub al-Mansur</b> defeated King Alfonso VIII of Castile, whose army retreated to Toledo, while the Almohads retook Trujillo, Montánchez and Talavera.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-18">18 July in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad Caliphate</a>. Died in Marrakech, in what is now <a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.63,-7.98&amp;year=1191" aria-label="Marrakech on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4344 1012 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Yaqub al-Mansur"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4794 1282h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4794 1282h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=31.63,-7.98&amp;year=1191">What was here in 1191? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
